Z||000010||fiero56||11-06-2000||03:22 PM||||Another weird oddity.<P>Got home from school today, and decided before I start preparations to remove the heads, I would take one last compression test of all cylinders, just for s&g. I connected the guage to each cylinder, exactly as before...Each cylinder is showing very close to 120psi, and none of them leak down, and there is no hissing in the cooling system. What is going on here?! <P>Earlier this morning when I performed the test, I took readings of each cylinder. Then, in the same order re-checked each cylinder a second time. I always had results exactly as I had written down. I even checked the rear bank(facing trunk)a third time and always had the same results.<P>I double check this time, and again have the same thing, 120psi, with no leak down. Maybe my "ghost" fixed the engine for me <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">. Stil doesn't explain what the "oil like" film was doing on the thermostat. Possibly really old dirty coolant?||||reg||1
Z||000011||ThatRickGuy||11-06-2000||04:29 PM||||Just for saftey sake, I'd say dump the oil and coolant, just to make sure you're not loosing fluids, and if there was anything that came across, well, coolant is bad for the drive train, so it's just a good idea. No idea on the ghost fix tho...||||reg||1
Z||000012||1FST2M6||11-06-2000||04:31 PM||||sorry I just re-read the post and kicked my self..   good luck... head gaskets are easy to replace.. only takes a weekend for a shadetree to do them...<p>[This message has been edited by 1FST2M6 (edited 11-06-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000013||Madrox86GT||11-06-2000||04:59 PM||||56...dude i hear you all the way...the same thing happened to me and my red SE...i handed over my keys to my dad and when i got home there was the SE in the yard with the lid open and my dad working on it...he said the belt broke on the way home....i might have killed it.  so hes been looking at it and realizing that it just might be a cracked head or just a cracked head gasket....we havent looked at in like 5-6 months because we have been so busy...dont know what the deal with it is...one day ill get out there and check it out...you may have just inspired me to do it now...mabey my ghost will help me out.<P>------------------<BR>You know my saying is:<P>"life is  a  B I T C H... then you marry one."<P>-Red 84 SE for parts(or 300 all together)-<BR>-Trade/Sell 86 GT-<BR>-New Pioneer Raido and 4X10's-<P>-Silver V6 SE(wrecked) $300<BR>-Black 85 coupe(parts car)$500<BR>-or both black and sliver for $750||||reg||1
Z||000014||88formula||11-06-2000||05:49 PM||||fiero56 I think your engine is fine. Those 2.8-liter engines have cast iron everything and cast iron is tough stuff. My friends have overheated small block fords and Chevy’s multiples times for way more than 2-miles and those engines still ran strong. A cheap way to check for leaking head gaskets is by just observing the coolant with the coolant cap off and the engine running fully warmed up. If you see lots of air bubbles coming up than you MAY have a leaking head gasket. Another telltale sign is lots of white smoke coming out of the exhaust.||||reg||1
Z||000015||Seanh||11-06-2000||06:01 PM||||Im in the process of removing and rebuilding the heads right now. Its not really as hard to do as it sounds. If your head gaskets are shot, 2 cylinders right next to each other with show low compression. Chances are, your car is fine. Did you do the compression test with the engine warm or cold?<P>------------------<BR><A HREF="http://www.kdsi.net/~sean" TARGET=_blank>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/seanhsig.gif">   <BR>Sean's Fiero Page</A><BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8 Auto<BR>Going on to 3.4 in Jan.<BR><I>If you smoke after sex, your doing it way to fast.</I>||||reg||1
Z||000016||Monkeyman||11-06-2000||06:33 PM||||Wish I could help you out, Drew.  If there's any way I can be of assistance (probably not at 160 miles away), let me know.||||reg||1
Z||000017||Seanh||11-06-2000||09:50 PM||||Just for the record, my 86 GT has a serpentine belt. It is not used for the reason they were created, it only uses the banded side, not the flat. I think all 2.8's have them, I could be wrong, but mine does.<P>------------------<BR><A HREF="http://www.kdsi.net/~sean" TARGET=_blank>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/seanhsig.gif">   <BR>Sean's Fiero Page</A><BR>Sean Heberly<BR>Silver 86 GT 2.8 Auto<BR>Going on to 3.4 in Jan.<BR><I>If you smoke after sex, your doing it way to fast.</I>||||reg||1
